//oc:doc "Prove a pending TOTP enrolment and arm the second factor (owner-gated).\n\nOn success the pending secret becomes the active one, the pending slot is cleared, and `/api/login` demands a code from the next login onward. Existing owner tokens are deliberately NOT revoked: the session doing this just proved the password (to log in) and now the code as well, so it is the most strongly authenticated session on the install — logging it out would be theatre.\n\n409 when there is no pending secret to prove, or when a factor is already active. A wrong code is 401 and leaves the pending secret intact for a retry."
//oc:route POST /api/auth/mfa/activate
//oc:id handle_mfa_activate_api_auth_mfa_activate_post
//oc:summary "Arm the second factor by proving a code from the pending secret."
//oc:body application/json req ref=MfaActivateDTO
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=MfaStateDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleMfaActivateApiAuthMfaActivatePost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
}

//oc:doc "Disarm the TOTP second factor (owner-gated), clearing the active secret, any pending secret and the replay floor.\n\nBOTH the current password and a live code are required. An owner-gated session is deliberately not enough on its own: a factor that a stolen session can switch off protects nothing after the session is stolen.\n\nThis is therefore NOT the lost-phone recovery path — an owner who cannot generate a code cannot use this endpoint at all. Recovery is the local `ocserverd mfa-disable` command, which substitutes proof of HOST SHELL ACCESS for proof of the factor. That is the same trust substitution the first-run claim token already makes, rather than a new backdoor.\n\n409 when no factor is active; 401 on a wrong password or code."
//oc:route POST /api/auth/mfa/disable
//oc:id handle_mfa_disable_api_auth_mfa_disable_post
//oc:summary "Turn the second factor off (password + live code required)."
//oc:body application/json req ref=MfaDisableDTO
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=MfaStateDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleMfaDisableApiAuthMfaDisablePost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
}

//oc:doc "Set the ship-dark feature flag `auth.mfa_offered` (owner-gated).\n\nOFF (the default, so an existing install is unaffected until its owner opts in) means `…/enroll` and `…/activate` answer 403 and the cockpit hides the entry.\n\n🔴 It NEVER affects verification. An already-armed factor keeps being demanded at login while this is false, `GET /api/auth/status` keeps reporting `mfa_required: true`, and `…/disable` keeps working — otherwise the flag would be a way for a stolen owner token to walk past the very factor that exists to stop it."
//oc:route POST /api/auth/mfa/offer
//oc:id handle_mfa_offer_api_auth_mfa_offer_post
//oc:summary "Turn the second-factor feature on or off for this server."
//oc:body application/json req ref=MfaOfferDTO
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=MfaStateDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleMfaOfferApiAuthMfaOfferPost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
}

//oc:doc "Mint a PENDING TOTP secret and return it once, with its `otpauth://` URI, so the owner can add it to an authenticator app (owner-gated).\n\nThis does NOT arm the second factor. The secret sits in a pending slot until `…/mfa/activate` proves a code generated from it; only then does it become active. That two-step shape is the whole safety property: enrolling straight into the active slot would let a mis-scanned QR lock the owner out of their own server on the very next login, with no way back except host shell access.\n\nCalling this again REPLACES any unproven pending secret (the previous one becomes unusable), so an owner who lost the enrolment screen simply starts over. It is refused with 409 while a factor is already active — rotating an armed secret means disabling it first, which requires proving the old one."
//oc:route POST /api/auth/mfa/enroll
//oc:id handle_mfa_enroll_api_auth_mfa_enroll_post
//oc:summary "Begin TOTP enrolment: mint a pending secret + otpauth URI."
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=MfaStateDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleMfaEnrollApiAuthMfaEnrollPost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
}

//oc:doc "First-time owner-password claim (PUBLIC, claim-token gated). The server mints\na one-shot claim token at first boot (no password set) and prints it ONLY to\nthe local serve log / installer banner; presenting it here proves host shell\naccess — the gate that stops a public-tunnel visitor from claiming a fresh\nserver. Order of checks: password already set → 409 (the token is never\nconsulted); claim token mismatch → 401; otherwise the argon2id hash is stored,\nthe claim token is deleted, and a fresh owner token is minted (the response\nlogs the caller straight in)."
//oc:route POST /api/auth/set-password
//oc:id handle_set_password_api_auth_set_password_post
//oc:summary "First-run: set the owner password (one-shot claim token gate)."
//oc:body application/json req ref=SetPasswordDTO
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=TokenDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleSetPasswordApiAuthSetPasswordPost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
}

//oc:doc "Pre-auth probe (PUBLIC): whether an owner password has been set, and whether\nthe login wall must also collect a TOTP code. The UI branches first-run setup\nvs login on `password_set` and renders its code field from `mfa_required`; see\nAuthStatusDTO for why that second bit is disclosed to an unauthenticated\ncaller. Nothing else about the auth state is disclosed — never the secret, the\nreplay floor, or how many verifications are in flight."
//oc:route GET /api/auth/status
//oc:id handle_auth_status_api_auth_status_get
//oc:summary "First-run probe: has the owner password been set?"
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=AuthStatusDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
