//oc:doc "List the server's HS256 signing keys (owner-gated).\n\nEvery key in the ring VERIFIES tokens; exactly one of them SIGNS new ones. This is what the settings page renders: how many keys exist, when each was created, and which one is signing.\n\n🔴 NO KEY MATERIAL, AND NOT EVEN A HASH OF IT, EVER APPEARS HERE. `key_id` is drawn from crypto/rand and stored; it is not a function of the key. A hash-derived identifier would be harmless for a random key and a password oracle for a migrated one, whose key IS `SHA-256` over the owner password (`deriveSecretFromPassword`) — publishing any digest of it would hand out an offline dictionary attack on that password.\n\n`created_ts` is `0` for the key an install has been using since before the ring existed: its creation time was never recorded, and clients MUST render that as unknown rather than as the epoch."
//oc:route GET /api/auth/signing-keys
//oc:id handle_signing_keys_api_auth_signing_keys_get
//oc:summary "List the signing keys: id, when it was made, which one signs."
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=SigningKeysDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleSigningKeysApiAuthSigningKeysGet(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
}

//oc:doc "Mint a new signing key and put it in charge of signing (owner-gated).\n\nThis is the transition, not a cut-over. The outgoing key STAYS in the ring and keeps verifying, so every token already in circulation — and every attachment share link — goes on working; it simply never signs again. Tokens minted from this point carry the new key.\n\nIt takes effect on the NEXT REQUEST: the ring is shared by pointer with the HTTP gate, so no restart and no redeploy is involved. (Within one process — a second server on the same database keeps its old ring until it restarts.)\n\nNothing is revoked here. Revocation is the separate, human-timed `…/{key_id}/remove`."
//oc:route POST /api/auth/signing-keys/rotate
//oc:id handle_signing_key_rotate_api_auth_signing_keys_rotate_post
//oc:summary "Mint a new signing key and hand signing over to it; the old one stays, verifying."
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=SigningKeysDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleSigningKeyRotateApiAuthSigningKeysRotatePost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
}

//oc:doc "Remove a retired signing key — THIS is the revocation, and it has no undo (owner-gated).\n\nThe moment this returns, every token signed by that key is refused, and every attachment share link produced under it stops working: a share `?sig=` is an HMAC under a key derived from the signing key, so it is governed by the ring too (owner ruling, card rc-cf9c27c07442). There is no grace period and holders are not notified — which is why the timing is a person's decision and never a timer's.\n\n⚠️ Warden credentials carry NO `exp`, so \"wait for the old tokens to expire\" is not a strategy for them: they are valid until their key leaves the ring. The question to answer before calling this is whether every machine has come back ON THE CURRENT KEY (`token_key_current` on GET /api/machines), not how many days have passed and not merely whether it reconnected: a machine that reconnected while still holding a credential this key signed drops off the moment this returns.\n\nThe key that is currently SIGNING cannot be removed (409) — rotate first, then remove the one that stepped down. An unknown `key_id` is a 404."
//oc:route POST /api/auth/signing-keys/{key_id}/remove
//oc:id handle_signing_key_remove_api_auth_signing_keys_key_id_remove_post
//oc:summary "Remove a retired key, revoking everything it signed. Refuses the signing key."
//oc:param path key_id req notitle
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=SigningKeysDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleSigningKeyRemoveApiAuthSigningKeysKeyIdRemovePost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request, keyId string) {
}

//oc:doc "Is the scheduled database backup still producing retreat points? (T-da06)\n\nThe backup engine already reported every outcome — but only to the server\nlog, a file with no reader, so \"the schedule died three days ago\" and \"all\ngood\" looked identical from the cockpit. This is the read side that makes the\ndifference visible: the cockpit renders the verdict under\nSettings › 系統更新與備份 (T-5e71, owner 2026-08-02 — it used to be a topbar\nindicator plus a monitor-page card, and he ruled both out).\n\nDeliberately its own small endpoint rather than a field on `GET /api/monitoring`:\nmonitoring is a large fold that re-fetches on every telemetry event, and this\nis a three-value answer read by a page that has nothing else to do with the\nmonitoring fold — hanging it there would move a big payload for no reason.\nThat separation is what makes it cheap to move the surface again.\n\nHonest by construction: a watchdog that has not evaluated yet reports\n`unknown`, never `healthy`."
//oc:route GET /api/backup-health
//oc:id handle_get_backup_health_api_backup_health_get
//oc:summary "Backup health: is the scheduled backup still producing retreat points?"
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=BackupHealthDTO "Successful Response"
