//oc:doc "Create one task type (a blank manual). Pass ``display_name``; the server mints the ``tm-`` type_key id and returns it in the DTO. Legacy: an explicit ``type_key`` is still accepted verbatim as the id (duplicate → 409; blank display_name backfills to it). Agent floor: any agent may create a manual; ``assignee`` is GOVERNANCE — a caller below admin_agent supplying it is a 403 (T-6020)."
//oc:route POST /api/task-manuals
//oc:id handle_create_task_manual_api_task_manuals_post
//oc:summary "Create a task type: pass display_name; the server mints and returns the tm- type_key id (legacy explicit type_key still accepted; duplicate → 409; assignee = owner/admin agent). An outsource assignee may select runtime claude/codex; absent = claude. Answers with a bounded receipt (``type_key``, ``updated_ts``, ``learnings_chars``, ``learnings_cap_chars``, ``learnings_sha256``, ``sop_md_chars``, ``sop_md_cap_chars``, ``sop_md_sha256``), not the manual — call ``get_task_manual`` when you need the rest."
//oc:body application/json req ref=TaskManualCreateDTO
//oc:resp 200 application/json ref=TaskManualReceiptDTO "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":true,"order":95,"name":"create_task_manual","description":"Create a task type: pass display_name; the server mints and returns the tm- type_key id (legacy explicit type_key still accepted; duplicate → 409; assignee = owner/admin agent). An outsource assignee may select runtime claude/codex; absent = claude. Answers with a bounded receipt (``type_key``, ``updated_ts``, ``learnings_chars``, ``learnings_cap_chars``, ``learnings_sha256``, ``sop_md_chars``, ``sop_md_cap_chars``, ``sop_md_sha256``), not the manual — call ``get_task_manual`` when you need the rest.","legacy":{"descriptor":"{\n      \"description\": \"Create a task type: pass display_name; the server mints and returns the tm- type_key id (legacy explicit type_key still accepted; duplicate \\u2192 409; assignee = owner/admin agent). An outsource assignee may select runtime claude/codex; absent = claude. Answers with a bounded receipt (``type_key``, ``updated_ts``, ``learnings_chars``, ``learnings_cap_chars``, ``learnings_sha256``, ``sop_md_chars``, ``sop_md_cap_chars``, ``sop_md_sha256``), not the manual \\u2014 call ``get_task_manual`` when you need the rest.\",\n      \"inputSchema\": {\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"assignee\": {\n            \"anyOf\": [\n              {\n                \"additionalProperties\": true,\n                \"type\": \"object\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"null\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"default\": null,\n            \"description\": \"Who executes tasks of this type. Supplying it at all is governance - a caller below admin-agent is a 403 - and the sub-keys the server KNOWS are checked properly: kind must be 'staff' or 'outsource' (400 otherwise), an outsource runtime, effort and copies are each validated against their own range, and an outsource machine must actually resolve. What is not checked is anything else you put in the object: unknown sub-keys are neither rejected nor read, just marshalled into the stored blob, so 'copys' or 'mashine' are accepted with 200 and then never take effect. TWO known keys also escape checking, and the second one is the expensive one. member_id is only tested for being non-empty, never looked up in the roster. model is never mentioned by the validator at all - yet it is what a spawn reads, and by two routes: every task this manual itself routes to outsource, AND an explicit target on create_task/reassign_task that left model unset on a task of this type, which inherits this one (the manual's model rides along when the two runtimes agree). So an unusable model name is stored with 200 and surfaces much later as a spawn that fails. A model that is not a string is not refused either: it is dropped as if you had never set it, and the field then falls through to the creator snapshot written on the task row when the task was created, reaching the launcher default only when that is empty too. Compare runtime and effort in this same object, which refuse a value they do not recognise.\",\n            \"title\": \"Assignee\"\n          },\n          \"display_name\": {\n            \"anyOf\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"null\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"default\": null,\n            \"description\": \"Human-readable label for the type. Whether a blank one is refused depends entirely on the OTHER field: with no type_key this is the only input there is, so blank is a 400; with a type_key supplied, a blank display_name is silently backfilled with that type_key and answers 200. The failure is therefore not an error message but a type list in which one row is labelled with its raw id while every other row carries a real name.\",\n            \"title\": \"Display Name\"\n          },\n          \"type_key\": {\n            \"anyOf\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"null\"\n              }\n            ],\n            \"default\": null,\n            \"description\": \"Stable id for the new task type, and the legacy compatibility path: supply it and it is taken verbatim as the id, an existing one being a 409. OMITTING it is not a validation error - the server mints an id of its own ('tm-' plus twelve hex characters) and creates the manual under that, 200 - so a caller who meant to set a memorable key ends up with a random one and finds out only by reading type_key back out of the response. On that minting path display_name becomes mandatory (blank is a 400); on the legacy path a blank display_name is silently backfilled with this key.\",\n            \"title\": \"Type Key\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"additionalProperties\": false,\n        \"type\": \"object\"\n      },\n      \"name\": \"create_task_manual\"\n    }"}}
func HandleCreateTaskManualApiTaskManualsPost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
}

