//oc:doc "PUBLIC webhook inlet (M4 §2). An external caller POSTs a free-text (untrusted) payload with the endpoint's opaque token as `?t=`. The server resolves (member · endpoint · purpose) SOLELY from the token — the path carries nothing else, so changing the path or any param cannot forge identity or purpose. An ENABLED token's payload is delivered to the member as ONE synthetic chat event (durable offline queue + online SSE, never auto-waking) carrying meta.webhook={endpoint_id,purpose}. An unknown/disabled/deleted token, an absent member, or a missing token all return the SAME silent 200 — the response never reveals whether an endpoint exists. Payload is size-capped; one POST yields at most one event. Verification depends on the endpoint's platform: `slack` first answers the url_verification challenge by echoing it synchronously, then verifies X-Slack-Signature; `github` verifies X-Hub-Signature-256; `generic` stays token-only. A request that fails verification is silently discarded."
//oc:route POST /in
//oc:id handle_receive_webhook_in_post
//oc:summary "Public webhook inlet — token-only (member/endpoint/purpose) delivery."
//oc:param query t union=[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]
//oc:resp 200 application/json inline={"title":"Response Handle Receive Webhook In Post","type":"object"} "Silent acknowledgement (identical for accepted and ignored)."
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleReceiveWebhookInPost(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request, t any) {
}

//oc:doc "Serve the one-line remote warden installer (``GET /install.sh``).\n\nA remote empty machine runs\n``curl -fsSL 'https://<server>/install.sh?code=<one-time code>' | bash``\nto install its warden in one step. The endpoint templates the request base_url +\nthe URL credential into the script body (see ``_build_install_script``)\nand returns it as ``text/plain`` so ``curl | bash`` streams it straight to a shell.\n\nAuth: PUBLIC. The script must be fetchable by an empty box with no header (the\ncredential rides in the URL, not an Authorization header). Exactly ONE of the two\nquery params must be present (neither, or both, is a 422):\n\n* ``code`` — the current onboard/boot-command surface: a short-lived (600 s),\n  single-use claim code. The served script redeems it FIRST (``POST\n  /api/machines/claim``, before any download) for the machine's real exec-token,\n  so a URL that leaks after use — or after 10 minutes — grants nothing.\n* ``token`` — the legacy surface, kept byte-identical indefinitely: the machine\n  exec-token itself is templated into the script (it authorizes the eventual\n  ``ocwarden install``, not the script fetch).\n\nThe binary the script pulls is served from the PUBLIC ``GET /api/warden/binary``.\nThe warden's runtime identity comes solely from the token's ``sub``."
//oc:route GET /install.sh
//oc:id handle_install_script_install_sh_get
//oc:summary "One-line remote warden installer (curl|bash; one-time code or legacy token in URL query)."
//oc:param query token union=[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]
//oc:param query code union=[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]
//oc:resp 200 application/json empty "Successful Response"
//oc:errors std
//oc:mcp {"include":false}
func HandleInstallScriptInstallShGet(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request, token any, code any) {
}

